IS  - 1080-6040 (Linking)
VI  - 20
IP  - 7
DP  - 2014 Jul
TI  - Independent lineages of highly sulfadoxine-resistant Plasmodium falciparum 
      haplotypes, eastern Africa.
PG  - 1140-8
LID - 10.3201/eid2007.131720 [doi]
AB  - Sulfadoxine-resistant Plasmodium falciparum undermines malaria prevention with 
      sulfadoxine/pyrimethamine. Parasites with a highly resistant mutant 
      dihydropteroate synthase (dhps) haplotype have recently emerged in eastern 
      Africa; they negated preventive benefits of sulfadoxine/pyrimethamine, and might 
      exacerbate placental malaria. We explored emerging lineages of dhps mutant 
      haplotypes in Malawi, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Tanzania by using 
      analyses of genetic microsatellites flanking the dhps locus. In Malawi, a 
      triple-mutant dhps SGEG (mutant amino acids are underlined) haplotype emerged in 
      2010 that was closely related to pre-existing double-mutant SGEA haplotypes, 
      suggesting local origination in Malawi. When we compared mutant strains with 
      parasites from the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Tanzania by multiple 
      independent analyses, we found that SGEG parasites were partitioned into separate 
      lineages by country. These findings support a model of local origination of SGEG 
      dhps haplotypes, rather than geographic diffusion, and have implications for 
      investigations of emergence and effects of parasite drug resistance.
