TI  - Three new single nucleotide polymorphisms identified in Plasmodium falciparum 
      isolates from Kogi State, Nigeria.
PG  - 117233
LID - S0732-8893(25)00555-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.diagmicrobio.2025.117233 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: High malaria burden remains a remarkable challenge in Nigeria. The 
      high prevalence has been attributed to a number of factors, including the 
      continued evolution of resistance to antimalarial drugs by Plasmodium. However, 
      the actual occurrence of antimalarial drug resistance in the country has not been 
      well-documented. This study was aimed at screening for molecular markers of 
      multidrug resistant Plasmodium species in individuals with parasitaemia in Kogi 
      State, Nigeria. METHOD: Blood samples drawn from 600 symptomatic patients who 
      consented to be recruited for the study were tested by Dipstick antigen test 
      (RDT) for malaria. Genomic DNA from 40 samples, confirmed positive for P. 
      falciparum by the rapid diagnostic test and PCR, were screened for the Pfmdr1 and 
      Kelch-13 genes. Some of the Kelch-13 PCR amplicons were randomly selected and 
      sequenced using Sanger method. Thereafter, the sequences were analysed and 
      compared with wild type for single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs). RESULTS: 
      Pfmdr1 and K-13 amplicons were successfully obtained from 45 % (18/40) of the 
      malaria-positive blood samples. Bioinformatics analysis of the K-13 sequences 
      showed near 100 % sequence homology and percentage similarity of 99.88 % with the 
      wild type Plasmodium falciparum 3D7 (Accession Number XM_001350122) gene. There 
      were however three non-synonymous mutations (K430E, W706C and Q707K) observed in 
      three samples. CONCLUSIONS: SNP analysis showed none of these mutations has been 
      previously associated with artemisinin resistance. The significance of the study 
      results was discussed in relation to current national malaria elimination and 
      antimalarial resistance strategic plan or policy.
